PR #226  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Backwards-incompatible redefinition of saturation immediate: PSATI/SATI width is uimm+1 while PUSATI/USATI width is uimm—code targeting the wrong spec version applies the incorrect offset, producing wrong saturation bounds and corrupting clamped arithmetic results.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/hwcap.h:13-128  (top-level macro definitions)
      Every RISCV_ISA_EXT_* constant is a bare integer logical ID with no spec-revision sub-field. The ISA bitmap is a flat bitmask that encodes only presence/absence per extension. When Zpn/Zpsfoperand are added, there is no mechanism to record which P-ext spec draft the hardware implements. The SATI uimm field changed from 'uimm' to 'uimm+1' between P-ext spec revisions —the same encoding has different saturation-width semantics depending on the revision— but the kernel's extension-ID infrastructure cannot distinguish between revisions. The implicit invariant that 'all harts and all software agree on a single fixed P-ext spec revision' is not enforced anywhere in code.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/cpufeature.c:492-593  (file-level table riscv_isa_ext)
      The riscv_isa_ext[] table registers each extension via __RISCV_ISA_EXT_DATA macros that map name → logical ID. Each entry tracks only whether the extension is present. There is no spec-revision field, no validation callback that could inspect P-ext draft version, and no mechanism to record that 'Zpn is present at draft X.Y.' If Zpn is added here, the kernel bakes in one spec revision's interpretation of SATI uimm encoding at compile time, with no runtime check against what the hardware actually implements.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:338-345  (top-level CSR macro definitions)
      CSR_VXSAT_MASK is defined as 1, a single-bit mask. This is architecturally correct for T-Head V-0.7.1 where vxsat is a single scalar saturation flag. However, if P-ext packed-SIMD SATI instructions reuse or alias this CSR space, a one-bit mask is insufficient to track per-element-lane saturation status. Multiple SIMD elements can saturate independently within one packed register, requiring a multi-bit vxsat field. The kernel currently has no provision for a wider mask, and any P-ext CSR aliasing would silently truncate per-element saturation state during context save/restore.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/vector.h:130-194  __vstate_csr_save / __vstate_csr_restore
      These paired functions correctly implement ISA spec-version dispatch for V-extension: has_xtheadvector() gates T-Head V-0.7.1 code (separate VXSAT/VXRM CSRs with FS_DIRTY workaround) vs standard V-1.0 (unified VCSR). This is the precise architectural pattern that P-ext SATI immediates would require —different spec revisions interpret the uimm field differently— but the kernel currently has no equivalent version-dispatch mechanism for P-ext. The vector code itself is correct and well-documented, with explicit fence-like csr_read_set/csr_write sequences protecting the FS toggle. It serves as a reference implementation of a solved problem that has not been replicated for P-ext.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/uapi/asm/kvm.h:119-199  (enum KVM_RISCV_ISA_EXT_ID)
      KVM's ISA extension enum is a flat enumeration with no spec-version sub-field. Each entry is just an incrementing integer identifier (A=0, C, D, F, ... ZALASR, KVM_RISCV_ISA_EXT_MAX). When Zpn/Zpsfoperand are added, the VMM can tell the guest 'Zpn is available' but cannot communicate which P-ext spec draft applies. If a VMM enables Zpn for a guest compiled against P-ext draft N while the host hardware implements draft M (with different uimm+1 convention), the guest will encode SATI immediates with incorrect saturation boundaries, producing silent data corruption with no architectural trap.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_onereg.c:28-110  kvm_isa_ext_arr / kvm_riscv_vcpu_set_reg_isa_ext
      kvm_isa_ext_arr[] maps KVM ISA extension IDs to kernel ISA extension IDs via KVM_ISA_EXT_ARR(ext) which expands to a 1:1 array index — no spec-version field. The riscv_vcpu_set_isa_ext_single() function (called from kvm_riscv_vcpu_set_reg_isa_ext at line 833) allows a VMM to set/clear any extension bit in vcpu->arch.isa before the VCPU first runs. When Zpn is added, a VMM can enable it for the guest with zero validation of spec-revision compatibility between host and guest. The only host check (kvm_riscv_vcpu_isa_check_host) verifies the host has the extension at all, not which draft version of the spec it follows.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_insn.c:328-359  kvm_riscv_vcpu_virtual_insn
      This is the virtual-instruction trap entry point. It decodes the trapping instruction's opcode and dispatches to system_opcode_insn() or truly_illegal_insn() (which forwards the trap to the guest VCPU). Currently, there is no P-ext-specific decode or emulation. If PSATI/PUSATI traps arrive (e.g., because Zpn is disabled for the guest), the instruction falls through to truly_illegal_insn() and the guest sees SIGILL. This is architecturally correct for 'extension not present.' However, if future in-kernel emulation of PSATI/PUSATI is added (to handle traps where Zpn is present but the instruction needs assistance), the emulator must decode the uimm field with the correct spec-revision-dependent +1 convention. No dispatch mechanism exists to select the correct emulation semantics based on the P-ext revision the guest was configured with.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/sys_hwprobe.c:81-175  hwprobe_isa_ext0
      hwprobe_isa_ext0() reports ISA extension availability to userspace via the EXT_KEY() macro, which calls __riscv_isa_extension_available() on the hart_isa bitmap. This yields a boolean: extension present or absent. When Zpn is reported as present, userspace receives a single bit (RISCV_HWPROBE_EXT_ZPN) with no accompanying spec-revision information. A userspace library or JIT compiled against a different P-ext spec revision than the hardware will encode SATI immediates with the wrong uimm convention (uimm vs uimm+1), producing incorrect saturation bounds that the hardware silently enforces — no trap, no fault, just wrong values.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/vendor_extensions/thead.h:6-20  (top-level definitions)
      The T-Head vendor extension framework (RISCV_ISA_VENDOR_EXT_XTHEADVECTOR=0, plus the riscv_isa_vendor_ext_list_thead infrastructure and the disable_xtheadvector() helper) is the kernel's existing mechanism for handling ISA spec version skew: it cleanly separates T-Head V-0.7.1 from standard V-1.0. The P-ext SATI immediate skew is structurally identical — different spec revisions interpret the same encoding differently — but the kernel currently has no vendor-ext or similar mechanism to encode P-ext spec sub-version in the extension bitmap. This is not a bug in the T-Head code (which is correct) but a gap: the proven pattern for version-skew handling is not being applied to P-ext.

